.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/cssesc Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "cssesc [![Build status](https://travis-ci.org/mathiasbynens/cssesc.svg?branch=master)](https://travis-ci.org/mathiasbynens/cssesc) [![Code coverage status](https://img.shields.io/codecov/c/github/mathiasbynens/cssesc.svg)](https://codecov.io/gh/mathiasbynens/cssesc)"
.PP
A JavaScript library for escaping CSS strings and identifiers while generating the shortest possible ASCII-only output\&.

.PP
This is a JavaScript library for \fRescaping text for use in CSS strings or identifiers\fP while generating the shortest possible valid ASCII-only output\&. \fRHere’s an online demo\&.\fP

.PP
\fRA polyfill for the CSSOM \fRCSS\&.escape()\fP method is available in a separate repository\&.\fP (In comparison, \fIcssesc\fP is much more powerful\&.)

.PP
Feel free to fork if you see possible improvements!
.SH "Installation"
.PP
Via \fRnpm\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install cssesc
.fi
.PP

.PP
In a browser:

.PP
.PP
.nf
<script src="cssesc\&.js"></script>
.fi
.PP

.PP
In \fRNode\&.js\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const cssesc = require('cssesc');
.fi
.PP

.PP
In Ruby using \fRthe \fRruby-cssesc\fP wrapper gem\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
gem install ruby\-cssesc
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
require 'ruby\-cssesc'
CSSEsc\&.escape('I ♥ Ruby', is_identifier: true)
.fi
.PP

.PP
In Sass using \fR\fRsassy-escape\fP\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
gem install sassy\-escape
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
body {
  content: escape('I ♥ Sass', $is\-identifier: true);
}
.fi
.PP
.SH "API"
.PP
.SS "\fRcssesc(value, options)\fP"
This function takes a value and returns an escaped version of the value where any characters that are not printable ASCII symbols are escaped using the shortest possible (but valid) \fRescape sequences for use in CSS strings or identifiers\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
cssesc('Ich ♥ Bücher');
// → 'Ich \\\\2665  B\\\\FC cher'

cssesc('foo 𝌆 bar');
// → 'foo \\\\1D306  bar'
.fi
.PP

.PP
By default, \fRcssesc\fP returns a string that can be used as part of a CSS string\&. If the target is a CSS identifier rather than a CSS string, use the \fRisIdentifier: true\fP setting (see below)\&.

.PP
The optional \fRoptions\fP argument accepts an object with the following options:
.SS "\fRisIdentifier\fP"
The default value for the \fRisIdentifier\fP option is \fRfalse\fP\&. This means that the input text will be escaped for use in a CSS string literal\&. If you want to use the result as a CSS identifier instead (in a selector, for example), set this option to \fRtrue\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
cssesc('123a2b');
// → '123a2b'

cssesc('123a2b', {
  'isIdentifier': true
});
// → '\\\\31 23a2b'
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRquotes\fP"
The default value for the \fRquotes\fP option is \fR'single'\fP\&. This means that any occurences of \fR'\fP in the input text will be escaped as \fR\\'\fP, so that the output can be used in a CSS string literal wrapped in single quotes\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
cssesc('Lorem ipsum "dolor" sit \\'amet\\' etc\&.');
// → 'Lorem ipsum "dolor" sit \\\\\\'amet\\\\\\' etc\&.'
// → "Lorem ipsum \\"dolor\\" sit \\\\'amet\\\\' etc\&."

cssesc('Lorem ipsum "dolor" sit \\'amet\\' etc\&.', {
  'quotes': 'single'
});
// → 'Lorem ipsum "dolor" sit \\\\\\'amet\\\\\\' etc\&.'
// → "Lorem ipsum \\"dolor\\" sit \\\\'amet\\\\' etc\&."
.fi
.PP

.PP
If you want to use the output as part of a CSS string literal wrapped in double quotes, set the \fRquotes\fP option to \fR'double'\fP\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
cssesc('Lorem ipsum "dolor" sit \\'amet\\' etc\&.', {
  'quotes': 'double'
});
// → 'Lorem ipsum \\\\"dolor\\\\" sit \\'amet\\' etc\&.'
// → "Lorem ipsum \\\\\\"dolor\\\\\\" sit 'amet' etc\&."
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRwrap\fP"
The \fRwrap\fP option takes a boolean value (\fRtrue\fP or \fRfalse\fP), and defaults to \fRfalse\fP (disabled)\&. When enabled, the output will be a valid CSS string literal wrapped in quotes\&. The type of quotes can be specified through the \fRquotes\fP setting\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
cssesc('Lorem ipsum "dolor" sit \\'amet\\' etc\&.', {
  'quotes': 'single',
  'wrap': true
});
// → '\\'Lorem ipsum "dolor" sit \\\\\\'amet\\\\\\' etc\&.\\''
// → "\\'Lorem ipsum \\"dolor\\" sit \\\\\\'amet\\\\\\' etc\&.\\'"

cssesc('Lorem ipsum "dolor" sit \\'amet\\' etc\&.', {
  'quotes': 'double',
  'wrap': true
});
// → '"Lorem ipsum \\\\"dolor\\\\" sit \\'amet\\' etc\&."'
// → "\\"Lorem ipsum \\\\\\"dolor\\\\\\" sit \\'amet\\' etc\&.\\""
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRescapeEverything\fP"
The \fRescapeEverything\fP option takes a boolean value (\fRtrue\fP or \fRfalse\fP), and defaults to \fRfalse\fP (disabled)\&. When enabled, all the symbols in the output will be escaped, even printable ASCII symbols\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
cssesc('lolwat"foo\\'bar', {
  'escapeEverything': true
});
// → '\\\\6C\\\\6F\\\\6C\\\\77\\\\61\\\\74\\\\"\\\\66\\\\6F\\\\6F\\\\\\'\\\\62\\\\61\\\\72'
// → "\\\\6C\\\\6F\\\\6C\\\\77\\\\61\\\\74\\\\\\"\\\\66\\\\6F\\\\6F\\\\'\\\\62\\\\61\\\\72"
.fi
.PP
.SS "Overriding the default options globally"
The global default settings can be overridden by modifying the \fRcss\&.options\fP object\&. This saves you from passing in an \fRoptions\fP object for every call to \fRencode\fP if you want to use the non-default setting\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
// Read the global default setting for `escapeEverything`:
cssesc\&.options\&.escapeEverything;
// → `false` by default

// Override the global default setting for `escapeEverything`:
cssesc\&.options\&.escapeEverything = true;

// Using the global default setting for `escapeEverything`, which is now `true`:
cssesc('foo © bar ≠ baz 𝌆 qux');
// → '\\\\66\\\\6F\\\\6F\\\\ \\\\A9\\\\ \\\\62\\\\61\\\\72\\\\ \\\\2260\\\\ \\\\62\\\\61\\\\7A\\\\ \\\\1D306\\\\ \\\\71\\\\75\\\\78'
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fRcssesc\&.version\fP"
A string representing the semantic version number\&.
.SS "Using the \fRcssesc\fP binary"
To use the \fRcssesc\fP binary in your shell, simply install cssesc globally using npm:

.PP
.PP
.nf
npm install \-g cssesc
.fi
.PP

.PP
After that you will be able to escape text for use in CSS strings or identifiers from the command line:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ cssesc 'föo ♥ bår 𝌆 baz'
f\\F6o \\2665  b\\E5r \\1D306  baz
.fi
.PP

.PP
If the output needs to be a CSS identifier rather than part of a string literal, use the \fR-i\fP/\fR--identifier\fP option:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ cssesc \-\-identifier 'föo ♥ bår 𝌆 baz'
f\\F6o\\ \\2665\\ b\\E5r\\ \\1D306\\ baz
.fi
.PP

.PP
See \fRcssesc --help\fP for the full list of options\&.
.SH "Support"
.PP
This library supports the Node\&.js and browser versions mentioned in \fR\fR\&.babelrc\fP\fP\&. For a version that supports a wider variety of legacy browsers and environments out-of-the-box, \fRsee v0\&.1\&.0\fP\&.
